  "description": "Implement a circular FIFO experience replay memory buffer with overwrite wrapping and batch mini-batch sampling.\n\nExperience replay breaks the temporal correlation of consecutive environmental states, satisfying the i.i.d. assumption of stochastic gradient descent.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = \\{ (s_i, a_i, r_i, s'_i, d_i) \\}_{i=1}^N, \\quad N \\le \\text{capacity}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Store transition tuples in sequential memory buffer up to capacity limit\n2. Wrap overwrite pointer circularly when capacity is saturated\n3. Draw mini-batches of transitions to decorrelate training data\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'buffer_size', 'sampled_batch', and 'is_full' boolean.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Experience Replay Buffer\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nIn reinforcement learning, there is no supervisor providing correct answers. Instead, an agent learns by trial and error: taking actions, observing environment states, and receiving feedback rewards. The agent must continuously balance exploiting known high-reward actions with exploring new actions that might be even better. **Experience Replay Buffer** implements a core decision-making or value-estimation algorithm.\n\nExperience replay breaks the temporal correlation of consecutive environmental states, satisfying the i.i.d. assumption of stochastic gradient descent. In this problem, the goal is to: implement a circular fifo experience replay memory buffer with overwrite wrapping and batch mini-batch sampling.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = \\{ (s_i, a_i, r_i, s'_i, d_i) \\}_{i=1}^N, \\quad N \\le \\text{capacity}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Store transition tuples in sequential memory buffer up to capacity limit\n2. **Phase 2**: Wrap overwrite pointer circularly when capacity is saturated\n3. **Phase 3**: Draw mini-batches of transitions to decorrelate training data\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Bellman Equations**: The core idea of dynamic programming in RL is that the value of a state equals the immediate reward plus the discounted expected value of the next state.\n* **Sample Efficiency**: Replay buffers and temporal difference updates allow agents to learn stably from past experiences.\n* **Policy Optimization**: Whether using value-based methods (like Q-learning) or policy gradients (like PPO), algorithms converge toward the optimal behavioral policy.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: capacity = 3, transitions_to_push = [{\"id\": 1}, {\"id\": 2}, {\"id\": 3}, {\"id\": 4}], sample_size = 2\n\nOutput: {\"buffer_size\": 3, \"sampled_batch\": [{\"id\": 4}, {\"id\": 2}], \"is_full\": 1}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated rl_experience_replay_buffer under standard test conditions."
